// claim-kind-alignment — the CURATED per-kind lexicon alignment (D68 §3).
//
// CHAIN-LOADED over the seeded lexicon (never bootstrap; its targets are seeded sense classes
// that exist only there), after encoding.esl. Each fact is a deliberate modeling statement —
// "a landed claim of kind K IS a <sense-class>" — and is what lets a demonstrative's restrictor
// («these findings» carries the lexicon's finding class) resolve to a kind-classed claim via the
// checker's ordinary multi-class inhabitation + subclass walk. NOTHING here is a new mechanism.
//
// SHADOWING REDECLARATION: each kind class from encoding.esl is redeclared here with its lexicon
// parents ADDED (the layered-resolution pattern the wordnet↔umls alignment established). The
// enc:Claim parent is restated — a redeclaration replaces, it does not merge.
//
// CURATION RECORD (2026-08-12, from the D68 restrictor probe over the page + labels):
//   aligned     — wn:n09279458  "finding: something that is found"        → Finding
//                 umlscui:C2825141 "Experimental Finding"                 → Finding
//                 wn:n01002956  "observation: the act of making and
//                                recording a measurement"                 → Observation
//                 umlscui:C0302523 "Observation in research"              → Observation
//                 umlscui:C5890437 "Observation (finding)"                → Observation
//                 wn:n01012712  "classification: the act of distributing
//                                things into classes"                     → Classification
//   NOT aligned — umlscui:C0037088 "Signs and Symptoms" (a clinical sign is not a claim);
//                 umlscui:C0700325 "Patient observation" (a clinical procedure);
//                 wn:n00151497 (the act-of-determining sense); wn:n00879759 (watching);
//                 wn:n07939638 (the arranged group); wn:n05732756 (the cognitive process);
//                 wn:n01148904 (government restriction).
//   Readings whose restrictor carries an unaligned sense simply do not resolve to claims —
//   the alignment doubles as sense discrimination, which is the point.
//
// ALIGNED `2026-08-20` — enc:Hypothesis, enc:Suggestion. Both were previously unaligned only
// because the WRN page never refers with «these hypotheses» / «these suggestions»; that is a
// property of one corpus, not of the vocabulary, and an unaligned kind fails the restrictor check
// SILENTLY (the demonstrative just does not resolve). Senses read from WordNet 3.0 data.noun:
//   hypothesis  n05888929 "a tentative insight into the natural world; a concept that is not yet
//                          verified but that if true would explain certain facts"   → Hypothesis
//               n07162545 "a proposal intended to explain certain facts or observations"
//                                                                                   → Hypothesis
//   suggestion  n05916155 "an idea that is suggested"                               → Suggestion
//               n07162680 "a proposal offered for acceptance or rejection"          → Suggestion
//   NOT aligned — wn:n06782680 (hypothesis as "a message expressing an opinion based on
//                 incomplete evidence" — the speech-act/conjecture reading, not the document's
//                 marked hypothesis); wn:n07246582 (persuasion formulated as a suggestion) and
//                 wn:n00158996 (inducing hypnosis) — the hypnotic senses; wn:n13773361 (a just
//                 detectable amount, "a suggestion of an accent"); wn:n05771679 (association of
//                 ideas). No UMLS parents: unlike finding/observation, these are discourse acts
//                 rather than clinical-data concepts, and UMLS's near matches are the wrong kind.
//
// enc:Assertion is deliberately unaligned FOREVER (an unmarked claim is not discourse-referable —
// fail-closed).

namespace enc     = "urn:eigenius:encoding";
namespace wn      = "urn:eigenius:wn";
namespace umlscui = "urn:eigenius:umlscui";

class enc:Finding : enc:Claim, wn:n09279458, umlscui:C2825141 {
    description = "A claim the document presents as an established result of its own investigation («These findings show …»). ALIGNED (this layer): wn finding = 'something that is found'; UMLS Experimental Finding.";
}

class enc:Observation : enc:Claim, wn:n01002956, umlscui:C0302523, umlscui:C5890437 {
    description = "A claim the document presents as something observed/measured («These observations suggest …»). ALIGNED (this layer): wn observation = 'the act of making and recording a measurement'; UMLS Observation in research; UMLS Observation (finding).";
}

class enc:Classification : enc:Claim, wn:n01012712 {
    description = "A claim assigning items to categories («These classifications were highly concordant …»). ALIGNED (this layer): wn classification = 'the act of distributing things into classes'.";
}

class enc:Hypothesis : enc:Claim, wn:n05888929, wn:n07162545 {
    description = "A claim the document marks as conjectured, typically by its matrix frame («We hypothesized that …»). Referable as a hypothesis; never as a finding. ALIGNED (this layer): wn hypothesis = 'a tentative insight … not yet verified'; wn hypothesis = 'a proposal intended to explain certain facts or observations'.";
}

class enc:Suggestion : enc:Claim, wn:n05916155, wn:n07162680 {
    description = "A claim the document presents as suggested/indicated rather than established («… suggest that …» as the matrix frame). ALIGNED (this layer): wn suggestion = 'an idea that is suggested'; wn suggestion = 'a proposal offered for acceptance or rejection'.";
}
